{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01Xv29xmPGyWJKjKRM8XKFN9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clear picture of what's needed. The design doc says to use Option A (extend the Backtest class with `run_replay()` method) and also to:\n\n1. Add `replay_source_run_id` field to `BacktestRun` model\n2. Create an alembic migration for this new field\n3. Implement `Backtest.run_replay()` method\n4. Update the UI in `apps/streamlit/views/replay.py` to actually call the replay\n\nThe previous run already tried this but ran out of time. Let me now plan exactly what to implement:\n\n\nI need to work through these four steps in order. First I'll add the new field to the model, then create the migration, implement the replay method, and finally wire up the UI. Looking at the design doc's guidance on signal loading, I should use date/asset/timeframe matching for the MVP—querying signals from a completed run using those existing fields. But I need to figure out how to identify which signals belong to the specific source run I'm replaying from.\n\nThe approach is to load the source BacktestRun and query its signals by matching the symbol against its assets list, the timeframe, and the generated_at timestamp against its date range, filtering for backtest environment signals. Then I'll create a new BacktestRun linked to the source via `replay_source_run_id`, iterate through the same date range, look up the stored signals for each date/asset pair, and apply the new position sizing and risk configuration before executing orders. Since replay just reuses existing signals, I don't need to regenerate them through a TradingGraph or strategy.\n\nThe main challenge is that `Backtest.__init__` constructs a full TradingGraph which requires LLM configuration, but for replay I only need the trading components like the portfolio, order manager, and risk monitor. I should refactor the initialization so I can set up replay mode without building the strategy graph. I'm setting up the `run_replay()` method to load the source run from the database, validate it exists, then reset the backtest instance variables like equity curve and trades before iterating through stored signals and executing orders with the new configuration.\n\nBuilding signal lookup map... Building the replay backtest run...\n\nNow I'm setting up the iteration loop to process each asset's date range, checking if we've moved to a new day to handle daily resets.\n\nFor each date, I'm executing the replay and trade logic while catching any errors, then recording the equity snapshot. Once all assets are processed, I calculate the final metrics, update the backtest record, and return the results.\n\nNow I'm looking up the stored signal for the given asset and date combination—if one exists, I'll use it directly instead of calling the LLM. If not found, I log it and move on. When a signal is available, I fetch the historical price data needed for execution.\n\nContinuing with the replay logic...\n\nIf there's no active position, I check the stored trading signal and skip if it's neutral, otherwise I proceed to execute a new order using the signal's direction and confidence level in backtest mode. Finishing the position monitor call with the filled quantity, then logging the execution. Now I'm realizing there's a potential issue with how signals are keyed during replay—the `generated_at` timestamp needs to match between the backtest and replay phases, which should work if we're using the same date range and timeframe, but I need to think through what happens if the portfolio manager generates signals differently.\n\nActually, the cleaner approach is to have the caller instantiate a Backtest with the target configuration already set (different initial capital, risk parameters, etc.), then call `run_replay()` with the source run ID. This way the replay just loads signals and skips the LLM generation step, keeping the logic simple. I'll need to add a `replay_source_run_id` field to the BacktestRun model to track which run we're replaying from.\n\nFor the implementation, I'm adding the foreign key relationship to BacktestRun, creating an alembic migration for the schema change, then implementing the replay methods in backtest.py. The tricky part is signal lookup—since Signal doesn't currently have a backtest_run_id field, I need to figure out how to match signals from the source run without accidentally pulling signals from other overlapping runs.\n\nThe MVP approach per the design doc is to match by date range, asset, and timeframe, but I'm realizing I should be more precise with the query filters to ensure we're only getting signals that fall within the source run's exact date range and match its specific assets and timeframe configuration.\n\nFor the signal lookup key, I'll use `(symbol, generated_at)` as the tuple since during replay the current date will align exactly with when the signal was generated, given we're using the same date range.\n\nOn the UI side, I need to wire up the replay button to actually execute `Backtest.run_replay()` with a loading spinner—since replay is fast without LLM calls, doing it synchronously in Streamlit should be fine for the MVP.\n\nNow I'm checking what imports are available in the replay view, and looking at how `_create_backtest_run()` works. It needs the source run's date range and assets but the target config, so I'll need to add a `replay_source_run_id` parameter to that method. The tricky part is that `_calculate_metrics()` currently filters trades by date range and environment, which would pull in trades from both the replay and source backtest if they share the same dates—I need to filter by `backtest_run_id` specifically to isolate the replay results.\n\nFor the MVP, since the replay runs immediately after the source completes and creates new trades with current timestamps, the metrics calculation should work fine as long as I'm careful about which backtest run ID I'm querying against.\n\nWait, I'm seeing the real issue now—when replaying on the same date range, both the original and replay trades would get picked up by the metrics query since they're both in the same time window. The trades are linked to signals through orders, and since replay reuses the stored signals rather than creating new ones, there's no way to distinguish which run produced which trade. I could filter by the signal ID, but that doesn't help since both runs use the same signals. The cleaner solution would be adding a `backtest_run_id` field to trades, but that's a bigger schema change than I want for MVP.\n\nActually, the pragmatic approach is simpler—since each replay creates a fresh Backtest instance, I could track which trades were created during that specific replay execution and only calculate metrics from those. The tricky part is that `_calculate_metrics()` queries the database directly rather than the in-memory portfolio state, so I'd need to modify it to accept a filter for trade IDs. Or I could rethink this entirely and base metrics on the equity curve instead of trade records, though that would require more refactoring than I want to do right now. solution is:\n1. Track replay-specific trades in memory during replay\n2. Calculate metrics from these in-memory trades rather than querying the DB\n\nOR: store `backtest_run_id` on the Signal (which is what comment 124 mentioned was done in the previous attempt), and use it to link trades.\n\nLet me look at what the previous run did vs what's in the current codebase:\n\nFrom comment 124: \"models.py modificado: campo `backtest_run_id` agregado a Signal con FK + índice + relationship bidireccional\"\n\nBut looking at the current `models.py`, there's no `backtest_run_id` field on Signal. This means the previous run's changes weren't committed.\n\nOK, let me think about the simplest, cleanest MVP implementation:\n\n**Approach**: \n1. Add `replay_source_run_id` to `BacktestRun`\n2. In `run_replay()`, calculate metrics from the equity curve (which is in-memory) rather than from the DB\n3. Use existing date/asset/timeframe filtering for signal lookup\n4. The UI will show replay results\n\nActually, I need to reconsider `_calculate_metrics()`. It already pulls Sharpe and drawdown from `self.equity_curve`, but it queries the DB for win rate and P&L. For those metrics to work correctly in a replay context, I need to filter trades to just this replay run.\n\nA simpler path: since the design uses the Backtest class with OrderManager and PositionMonitor components that operate on shared DB state, I should create a `run_replay()` method that instantiates a fresh BacktestRun with `replay_source_run_id`, loads source signals from the DB, iterates through dates using stored signals instead of the LLM, and tracks the resulting trades to calculate metrics.\n\nThe key challenge is isolating which trades belong to this replay run. I could collect the Order objects created during replay and filter trades by their order IDs.\n\nFor the MVP, I'll add a `_replay_trade_ids` set to track trades during replay, then pass it to `_calculate_metrics()` to filter the results. This keeps the solution minimal and focused on what's needed.\n\nQuerying the trades...\n\nThe issue is that during replay, using the source run's date range would pull in trades from all backtest runs in that period, not just the current replay. I need to track which trades belong to this specific replay by collecting their IDs as they're created, then filtering by those IDs in the metrics calculation instead of relying on dates.\n\nThere's also the portfolio manager state to consider—if the Backtest instance was reused from a previous run, its in-memory positions would be stale. But since we're creating a fresh instance for each replay, that shouldn't be an issue.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the implementation: I'll add a `replay_source_run_id` column to track which run a replay came 